sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch10-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Small ciliated and flagellated organisms cannot steer, but by rotating about their long axis while swimming they convert a constant body-fixed swerve into a continually changing direction in space, so they travel along a helix around a nearly straight axis rather than in a circle.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Small swimmers rotate about their axis, turning a constant swerve into a helical path";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter X, “A Parenthetic Note on Geodetics”; topic “Spiral paths of swimming microorganisms”. Scope: ciliates, flagellates, rotifers, swarm-spores and other small swimming organisms"
}
